  "_id": "6682bd2aba0e94a3c4025aed",
  "type": "numerical",
  "level": 3,
  "questionLabels": [],
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>The product of the following reaction is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math>.</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/8cb3977c-f1ee-440a-9bc0-5fed45f8b257.png\" style=\"width: 400px; height: 229px;\"/></p><p>The number of hydroxyl groups present in the product <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> is________.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "0",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The reaction proceeds via addition of Grignard's reagent (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>PhMgBr</mi></math>).</p><p>A Grignard reagent is an organomagnesium compound which can be described by the chemical formula ‘R-Mg-X’<em> </em>where R refers to an alkyl or aryl group and X refers to a halogen.</p><p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mi>CHOOH</mi></math> reacts with grignard's reagent  benzene along with an addition product is formed, which on further reaction with aq ammonium chloride gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mi>CHOOH</mi></math>.</p><p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Ph</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math> is a strong base which is extracting proton  from the reactant to give benzene.</p><p>The reaction is as follows,</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/0b000aae-f856-49e4-816b-866b12f231d7.png\" style=\"width: 752px; height: 246px;\"/></p><p>Product P is benzene and it has zero hydroxyl group.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
